q=gap(100,'Integrated original YOLO decode and suppression','Use the original-YOLO coordinate convention with a supplied 4×4 grid on a 400×400 pixel image. Cells are zero-indexed as (column,row). A and B lie in cell (1,1), whose conditional person probability is 0.8: A=(x=0.8,y=0.5,w=0.5,h=0.5,box confidence=0.9); B=(0.9,0.5,0.5,0.5,0.8). C lies in cell (2,1), whose conditional person probability is 0.5: C=(0.4,0.5,0.25,0.25,0.7). Centers are cell-relative; widths/heights are whole-image fractions. Test only the person class. Keep class-specific scores strictly above 0.4, then apply descending-score greedy NMS, suppressing a lower-ranked box if its IoU with a kept box is greater than 0.5. Which tuple gives A’s pixel corners (left,top,right,bottom), A’s person score, and the final survivors?',[
('A corners (80,50,280,250); A score 0.72; survivors {A}.','A decodes to a 200×200 box centered at (180,150). Class scores are 0.72, 0.64 and 0.35, so C is filtered. A/B IoU is 19/21, so the higher-score A suppresses B.'),
('A corners (155,125,205,175); A score 0.72; survivors {A}.','These corners incorrectly scale widths/heights by the 100-pixel cell instead of the 400-pixel image. That wrong geometry still happens to suppress B, but it is not the stated convention.'),
('A corners (80,50,280,250); A score 0.90; survivors {A,C}.','This uses box confidence without multiplying the conditional class probability. It incorrectly lets C pass; with correct scoring, C has only 0.35 and is removed before NMS.'),
('A corners (80,50,280,250); A score 0.72; survivors {A,B}.','A/B overlap is 38,000 pixels² and their union is 42,000 pixels². Dividing by the sum of areas without subtracting the intersection gives the wrong 0.475 ratio and wrongly retains B.')],[cite(1075,'statement','Bounding-box coordinate and confidence definitions','The box center is relative to the grid cell; width and height are relative to the whole image.'),cite(1075,'equation','Intersection-over-union definition','IoU = intersection area / union area.'),cite(1076,'statement','Class-probability and NMS steps','Class probability prediction is per grid cell; NMS retains the high-confidence detection.')],'hard',['All numeric candidates, grid size, thresholds and person-only policy are supplied illustrative values; this is an original-YOLO convention example, not a YOLOv8 raw-output decoder.','The given box confidence already includes the original model’s objectness/estimated-localization confidence; do not multiply it by objectness again.','No other candidates or classes are considered. The origin is top-left; x increases rightward and y downward; axis-aligned continuous-coordinate boxes use width right−left, with no +1 pixel correction or clipping.','Class-specific score equals the cell’s conditional person probability times the candidate’s box confidence. This is a modeled score, not an independently calibrated correctness probability.'])
q['solution']['steps']=['Each cell side is 400/4=100 pixels. A center is ((1+0.8)100,(1+0.5)100)=(180,150), with width=height=0.5×400=200. Its corners are (80,50,280,250).','B center is (190,150), corners (90,50,290,250). C center is (240,150), width=height=100, corners (190,100,290,200).','Person scores: A=0.8×0.9=0.72, B=0.8×0.8=0.64, C=0.5×0.7=0.35. Only A and B exceed 0.4.','A/B intersection is 190×200=38,000 pixels². Each box area is 40,000 pixels², so union=40,000+40,000−38,000=42,000 pixels².','IoU=38,000/42,000=19/21≈0.905>0.5. Keep A first and suppress B; C was already removed.','For the box-confidence-only distractor, A/C IoU=9,000/(40,000+10,000−9,000)=9/41≈0.220, explaining why that incorrect score rule would leave C unsuppressed.']
